{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#2621","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#2621","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#2621\",\"parent_revision\":\"c7b3426cda4afa46323a2fba649435a20d8e5656\",\"root_revision\":\"d5410cb1916b5449434f71f798a730a8c551d5b29fd521805d677daf4372d7e5\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P2\\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-12\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nThe complexity-fit formula `(knowledge + uncertainty + context overhead) / available context` is documented in `docs/complexity-fit-and-economics-of-agents.md` and referenced in CLAUDE.md. It is intended to help the orchestrator decide when a task is too complex for one agent and should be decomposed.\\n\\nVerified by G7 complexity-routing layer (2026-06-08): zero grep hits for the formula or its components in any skill file under `skills/`. The formula exists only in design documents. No skill flowchart reads a complexity score and branches on it. No gate evaluates it before dispatch.\\n\\nImpact: the formula is a design intent that has not been wired into the system. Work sizing decisions are made without a structured complexity signal, defaulting to the orchestrator's judgment.\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Work matches description\\n- [ ] Plan or implementation complete\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **developer using Claude Code skills**, I want to **complexity-fit formula is designed-only \u2014 no skill evaluates it to gate or size work** so that **the tooling becomes more capable and complete**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: G7 complexity-routing enforcement layer, workflow-map audit 2026-06-08\\n- **Priority**: P2\\n- **Added**: 2026-06-08\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## Groomed (2026-06-09)\\n\\n### RT-ICA Assessment\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-09T08:27:53.434327Z</sub>\\n\\n- Goal: Wire the complexity-fit formula into at least one skill as a real conditional gate or sizing signal, so the formula changes observable dispatch or decomposition behavior\\n- Status: APPROVED-WITH-GAPS\\n- AVAILABLE:\\n  - Formula definition: `docs/sdlc-layers/layer-0/context-fit-complexity.md` \u2014 canonical equation with three cost components (required non-native knowledge, active uncertainty, live context overhead)\\n  - Design document: `docs/complexity-fit-and-economics-of-agents.md` \u2014 11 claims with experiment designs\\n  - Adoption conditions: `docs/complexity-fit-adoption-conditions.md` \u2014 per-concept apply/skip criteria\\n  - G7 enforcement audit: `docs/workflow-layers/G7-complexity-routing.json` \u2014 gap entry `G7.complexity-fit-model` with enforcement_status = designed-only and explicit gap description\\n  - Integration candidates: `skills/task-decomposition/SKILL.md` step 6 Gate node (exists but has no procedure), `skills/planner-rt-ica/SKILL.md` Complexity Model section (definitional only)\\n- DERIVABLE:\\n  - Which skills are primary integration points: derivable from G7 gap entry and layer-0 doc relationships \u2014 task-decomposition step 6 and planner-rt-ica Complexity Model section are the named candidates\\n  - What qualitative routing criteria exist: derivable from adoption conditions doc \u2014 apply/skip signals per concept map to low/medium/high knowledge cost routing\\n- MISSING:\\n  - Scope of this item: one integration point (task-decomposition) or multiple (planner-rt-ica, dispatch, planning) \u2014 not specified\\n  - Numeric vs qualitative threshold: design docs provide qualitative apply/skip criteria but no ratio cutoff; planner must decide before writing flowchart branches\\n  - Whether autonomy_mode selection should be driven by this formula: not answered in any current skill file\\n</div>\\n\\n### Artifact Classification\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-09T08:28:12.187623Z</sub>\\n\\n- Primary artifact type: Skill\\n- Behavioral boundary: An agent using the updated skill evaluates the formula components before dispatching a task or recommending decomposition \u2014 the observable output is a different dispatch path, task split, or blocking question depending on the evaluated score\\n- Domain-semantic evaluation target: Acceptance criteria must prove that a skill flowchart branches on formula output, not merely that the formula is mentioned in prose\\n</div>\\n\\n### Reproducibility\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-09T08:28:35.161824Z</sub>\\n\\n1. Run `grep -r 'complexity.fit\\\\|knowledge.*uncertainty\\\\|available.context\\\\|context.*overhead' plugins/development-harness/skills/` from the repo root\\n2. Observe: zero matches in any skill SKILL.md file (one hit in evaluate-sdlc-layers is a reference, not an evaluation gate)\\n3. Read `plugins/development-harness/skills/planner-rt-ica/SKILL.md` \u2014 the Complexity Model section states the definition but contains no flowchart branch or conditional action\\n4. Read `plugins/development-harness/skills/task-decomposition/SKILL.md` \u2014 step 6 Gate node says 'Evaluate complexity' with branches 'Manageable' and 'High complexity or novel architecture' but provides no procedure for how to evaluate it\\n5. Observe: no skill file contains an instruction to compute (knowledge + uncertainty + context overhead) / available context and act on the result\\n</div>\\n\\n### Output / Evidence\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-09T08:29:11.320791Z</sub>\\n\\n- G7 enforcement audit entry `G7.complexity-fit-model` in `plugins/development-harness/docs/workflow-layers/G7-complexity-routing.json` with `enforcement_status: designed-only` and explicit gap: \\\"No skill flowchart evaluates the ratio and changes dispatch path, autonomy_mode, or agent tier based on it.\\\"\\n- Grep confirmation: `grep -r 'complexity.fit' plugins/development-harness/skills/` returns one hit in `evaluate-sdlc-layers/SKILL.md` \u2014 a reference check, not an evaluation gate\\n- `task-decomposition/SKILL.md` step 6 Gate node exists but contains no formula evaluation procedure; agents reading it interpret 'Evaluate complexity' as undirected judgment\\n- `planner-rt-ica/SKILL.md` Complexity Model section is definitional prose \u2014 it describes the ratio concept but contains no conditional instruction routing the agent based on the score\\n- `docs/complexity-fit-and-economics-of-agents.md` exists as a design/claims document with experiment designs \u2014 it is not a skill file\\n</div>\\n\\n### Priority\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-09T08:29:51.420039Z</sub>\\n\\n5/10 \u2014 The formula is documented design intent surfaced by a formal G7 audit. The gap is real and the evidence is unambiguous. Impact is bounded: work-sizing decisions default to orchestrator judgment rather than a structured signal, which is the current working baseline. No blocker depends on this item. P2 is correct.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Impact\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-09T08:30:23.417823Z</sub>\\n\\n- Blocks: nothing is directly blocked by this gap\\n- Bottleneck: orchestrators making decomposition and dispatch decisions rely on informal judgment rather than a structured signal; borderline tasks are sized ad hoc\\n- Related gap: G7.autonomy-mode (documented in G7-complexity-routing.json) \u2014 autonomy_mode defaults to full_auto with no item-level signal governing the choice; this formula could be the missing input for that selection\\n- Related item: #1855 (token_budget field in Task/Plan models) \u2014 a token-budget-aware dispatch would benefit from the complexity-fit score as the sizing input\\n</div>\\n\\n### Benefits\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-09T08:30:53.223026Z</sub>\\n\\n- Orchestrators and planners get a structured procedure for deciding when to split a task versus dispatch as-is, replacing undirected judgment\\n- task-decomposition step 6 becomes a verifiable gate rather than an undefined 'evaluate' instruction \u2014 agents can cite the procedure rather than guessing\\n- autonomy_mode selection could be governed by formula output (high cost \u2192 per_task or checkpoint; low cost \u2192 full_auto), resolving the G7.autonomy-mode gap\\n- The adoption conditions doc provides ready-made apply/skip criteria that can be lifted directly into skill flowchart branches\\n</div>\\n\\n### Expected Behavior\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-09T08:31:25.289913Z</sub>\\n\\nWhen a skill instructs an agent to evaluate the complexity-fit formula:\\n\\n1. The agent identifies the three cost components for the task at hand: required non-native knowledge (project-specific files, schemas, conventions that must be loaded), active uncertainty (MISSING RT-ICA conditions), and live context overhead (transient content already in the window)\\n2. The agent estimates whether the sum of these costs leaves enough working context to execute reliably\\n3. Based on the evaluation, the agent takes a documented branch:\\n   - Low cost: proceed as single agent\\n   - Medium/borderline: check whether two steps share the same knowledge payload and can be combined; if not, proceed with explicit knowledge-loading plan\\n   - High cost: decompose at knowledge boundaries \u2014 each subtask receives a smaller knowledge subset\\n4. The branching decision is observable \u2014 it appears as a decomposition split, a dispatch mode selection (per_task vs full_auto), or a blocking question, not silent execution\\n5. The evaluation procedure does not require computing a numeric ratio; qualitative criteria from the adoption conditions doc are sufficient\\n</div>\\n\\n### Desired Structure\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-09T08:31:56.214253Z</sub>\\n\\nAt minimum one skill file contains a flowchart branch or procedure that:\\n- Instructs the agent to enumerate knowledge cost (what project-specific context must be loaded), uncertainty cost (unresolved RT-ICA MISSING conditions), and overhead cost (transient content already consuming window budget)\\n- Provides qualitative criteria for 'fits single agent' vs 'should split at knowledge boundary' derived from `docs/complexity-fit-adoption-conditions.md`\\n- Routes to a documented outcome (proceed / split / escalate) rather than leaving evaluation undefined\\n\\nThe integration point is most naturally `task-decomposition/SKILL.md` step 6 Gate node, which already has 'Evaluate complexity' as a placeholder. The Gate node needs procedure text, not an entirely new section.\\n\\nA secondary integration in `planner-rt-ica/SKILL.md` (Complexity Model section made actionable with a recommended action per cost tier) is optional scope for the same pass.\\n\\nThe formula does NOT need to produce a numeric score. Qualitative bucketing (low / medium / high context cost) is sufficient. The adoption conditions doc provides the ready-made apply/skip criteria for each cost tier.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-09T08:32:29.410877Z</sub>\\n\\n1. `grep -r 'knowledge.*cost\\\\|uncertainty.*cost\\\\|overhead.*cost\\\\|knowledge.*boundary\\\\|split.*knowledge\\\\|complexity.fit' plugins/development-harness/skills/` returns at least one match inside a procedure or flowchart section of a skill SKILL.md file (not a reference link or comment)\\n2. Given the current `task-decomposition/SKILL.md` step 6 Gate node (\\\"Evaluate complexity\\\"), the gate contains a procedure or criteria list that tells the agent how to evaluate complexity using the formula components \u2014 not undirected judgment\\n3. Given a task described as requiring loading 5+ project-specific files before any edit can be made (high knowledge cost), an agent following the updated skill routes to decompose at knowledge boundaries rather than dispatching as a single unit\\n4. Given a task described as pure algorithmic work with all required context in training weights (zero knowledge cost), an agent following the updated skill confirms proceed as single agent\\n5. The evaluation criteria are grounded in `docs/complexity-fit-adoption-conditions.md` apply/skip signals \u2014 not invented by the skill author; the relevant source section can be cited\\n6. The skill change does not introduce a numeric threshold (the formula is qualitative at this stage) \u2014 criteria use observable conditions such as file count to load, number of MISSING RT-ICA conditions, and current window consumption\\n</div>\\n\\n### Questions for Human\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-09T08:34:36.394853Z</sub>\\n\\n- Should this item be scoped to task-decomposition step 6 only (Small), or should it also cover planner-rt-ica Complexity Model in the same pass (Medium)?\\n- Is qualitative bucketing (low / medium / high knowledge cost) an acceptable substitute for a numeric ratio threshold, or does the formula need a numeric cutoff to be useful?\\n</div>\\n\\n### Resources\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-09T08:32:59.192276Z</sub>\\n\\n| Type | Item |\\n|------|------|\\n| Docs | plugins/development-harness/docs/sdlc-layers/layer-0/context-fit-complexity.md |\\n| Docs | plugins/development-harness/docs/complexity-fit-and-economics-of-agents.md |\\n| Docs | plugins/development-harness/docs/complexity-fit-adoption-conditions.md |\\n| Docs | plugins/development-harness/docs/workflow-layers/G7-complexity-routing.json (entry G7.complexity-fit-model) |\\n| Skill | /dh:task-decomposition (primary integration candidate \u2014 step 6 Gate node) |\\n| Skill | /dh:planner-rt-ica (Complexity Model section \u2014 definitional, actionable integration candidate) |\\n| Skill | /dh:planning (step 2 Solution Design) |\\n| Skill | /dh:dispatch (two dispatch modes flowchart) |\\n| Backlog | #1855 \u2014 token_budget field in Task/Plan models (related: context budget signal) |\\n</div>\\n\\n### Dependencies\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-09T08:33:20.996193Z</sub>\\n\\n- Depends on: None \u2014 the formula definition, adoption conditions, and G7 gap evidence are all AVAILABLE; no prerequisite items must complete first\\n- Blocks: G7.autonomy-mode gap (undocumented in backlog) could be partially resolved once the formula is wired as a sizing signal; #1855 (token_budget) benefits from this formula as the complexity input\\n</div>\\n\\n### Blockers\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-09T08:33:45.082850Z</sub>\\n\\n- Scope is undefined: the item does not specify whether one integration point (task-decomposition step 6) or multiple skills (planner-rt-ica, dispatch, planning) should be addressed in one pass \u2014 planner should constrain scope before implementation begins\\n- Qualitative threshold: no numeric cutoff exists; qualitative apply/skip criteria from adoption conditions doc must substitute \u2014 this is acceptable but the planner must confirm this approach before writing flowchart branches\\n- Skill structural change: planner-rt-ica Complexity Model is definitional prose without a Mermaid flowchart; if that skill is in scope, the structural change requires plugin-creator:skill-creator validation to avoid introducing frontmatter or progressive-disclosure violations\\n</div>\\n\\n### Effort\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-09T08:34:15.265516Z</sub>\\n\\nSmall to Medium \u2014 the formula definition and adoption conditions are fully documented; all source material is AVAILABLE. The work is skill editing: add a procedure or criteria list to task-decomposition step 6 and optionally planner-rt-ica Complexity Model. No new docs, no new code, no schema changes. Primary risk is scope creep (one skill vs four). Constraining to task-decomposition step 6 first keeps this Small. Adding planner-rt-ica as a second integration point in the same pass is Medium.\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"d793c584e585246757b766c6cd7ac868d4e1d4d48942d85b92252b663f337a45\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABPnMysA\"}"}